@@ -5,11 +5,11 @@ An explainable crop-suitability screen linked to CropRetain's existing market co
 ## What it does
 `POST /soil/advise` takes any of: `soil_ph`, `soil_texture`, `season`, `irrigation`, and optional Soil Health Card values (`nitrogen_kg_ha`, `phosphorus_kg_ha`, `potassium_kg_ha`, `organic_carbon_pct`, `ec`); at least one soil input is required. Optional extras: the pair `quantity_kg` + `location`, `values_from_card` (see below) and `top_n` (1-10, caps how many assessed crops are returned; the response reports `recommendations_total`).
 
-For each crop that has cited rules (**Paddy (Rice), Soybean, Chana (Gram)**) it returns, check by check, whether the input falls inside what the cited sources report, with the source for each check. There is no invented score: results read "2 of 3 checks matched".
+For each crop that has cited rules (**Paddy (Rice), Soybean, Chana (Gram), Wheat, Maize, Groundnut**) it returns, check by check, whether the input falls inside what the cited sources report, with the source for each check. There is no invented score: results read "2 of 3 checks matched".
 
 If `quantity_kg` and `location` are given, each crop also carries the estimated net return from the **existing** `market._compare_markets_core` → `transport_optimizer.net_profit_breakdown` path. The Soil Advisor adds no financial calculation of its own.
 
-`GET /soil/options` lists the allowed input values and known locations.
+`GET /soil/options` lists the allowed input values and known locations, plus `texture_guide` and `texture_guide_sources` for the soil-type picker.
 
 ## Soil Health Card values and input confidence
 Nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium, organic carbon and EC are classified into the published fertility classes (low / medium / high; EC classes) by `services/soil_health_card.py` and returned as `soil_health`. Each value is classified **under every cited source** (ICAR-IISS Soil Health Card article; TNAU soil rating chart). The two sources use different limits for N, P, K and EC, so they are kept as separate claims and `sources_disagree` is set where they give different classes; nothing is averaged.
@@ -39,7 +39,17 @@ Rules live in `backend/app/services/soil_crop_rules.py`; every claim has a sourc
 - Soybean's texture claim "sandy loam to clayey" is expanded to explicit classes by us and flagged `interpreted`.
 - Paddy has no season rule (none was verified), so season is reported as not assessed for paddy.
 - Water requirements are shown as notes with their source, never pass/fail, because mapping "rainfed/limited/assured" to millimetres would need an invented conversion.
-- The other 7 catalogue crops are `UNAVAILABLE` until a citable source is added to `soil_crop_rules.py`.
+- **Wheat and Maize have no pH rule**: no source read gives a numeric pH range (the maize bulletin says only "neutral pH is good"), so pH is reported as not assessed for them.
+- Wheat, Maize and Groundnut sources were read on 2026-10-19. Only the Raigarh (Chhattisgarh) contingency plan, used for wheat's season, is Chhattisgarh-specific; the rest are TNAU, Assam, ICAR-DMR and ICRISAT/Odisha material. Wheat's two water sources disagree (TNAU 4-6 irrigations vs Assam's two) and both are shown.
+- "Spring" and "summer" sowings are mapped to our `zaid` season, and "loamy sand" / "silty loam" are mapped to the nearest of our texture classes; both are flagged as interpreted in the claim labels.
+- Tomato, Potato, Onion and Sugarcane remain `UNAVAILABLE` until a citable source is added to `soil_crop_rules.py`.
+
+## Soil-type picker
+The texture dropdown is replaced by visual cards (`components/SoilTexturePicker.jsx`) with a first-class "Not sure / skip" card that simply skips the texture check. Each card shows a hand-test hint: whether moist soil holds a ball, how long a ribbon it makes (loam group up to 2.5 cm, clay-loam group 2.5-5 cm, clayey group over 5 cm), and whether it feels gritty or smooth, plus "sticky when wet" for clay. These come from `TEXTURE_GUIDE` in `soil_crop_rules.py` and are cited to the FAO Global Soil Partnership ribbon exercise (method attributed to USDA-NRCS) and a Kansas State extension note, read 2026-10-19.
+- The pictures are schematic sketches of mixed particle sizes, drawn by us. They are not photos and not lab results, and the card says so.
+- FAO's table calls clay "neither gritty nor smooth" while its notes call fine soils "very smooth and sticky"; because that is unclear, no gritty/smooth claim is made for clay.
+- Both sources are international (FAO, US extension), not Indian. A hand test is an estimate; the picker tells farmers to choose "Not sure" or ask the local KVK.
+- Accessibility: native radio inputs in one group (arrow keys work), visible focus ring, illustrations are `aria-hidden`. The text uses `soil.picker.*` and `soil.guide.*` keys; they are English-only for now.
 
 ## Not included
 Photo- or sensor-based soil detection, crop-specific nutrient requirements or fertiliser doses, district-specific rules or regional soil estimates, yield prediction, and cultivation-cost accounting (net return excludes seed, fertiliser, labour and irrigation costs).
